From 2d409cf9aa119d3e8b0770d7b5b581ad6650cb1a Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Stefan Hertenberger <erde74@gmail.com>
Date: Fri, 27 Nov 2026 09:40:39 +0100
Subject: [PATCH] mountfix: buf and nbuf are volatile (allocated after
 waserror, freed after its handler)

mountfix allocates buf after its waserror() and may free and allocate
it again (when the stat and the new name need more than nbuf); an error there (Eshortstat,
the device's stat) goes to Norewrite, the loop goes on with the next
entry, and buf is freed at the end.  Without volatile, buf and nbuf are
indeterminate after the jump back (C11 7.13.2.1, as 0066): a compiler
that keeps them in registers may hand back the buf freed before the
second smalloc (used by the next entry's stat, then freed again) or nil
(the allocation leaks).  Found by a review of the namec volatile patch
(2026-11-27); compiled with gcc and clang, not run.

Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5.5 <noreply@anthropic.com>
---
 sys/src/9/port/sysfile.c | 10 ++++++++--
 1 file changed, 8 insertions(+), 2 deletions(-)

diff --git a/sys/src/9/port/sysfile.c b/sys/src/9/port/sysfile.c
index 76b2e071f..72faac684 100644
--- a/sys/src/9/port/sysfile.c
+++ b/sys/src/9/port/sysfile.c
@@ -557,14 +557,20 @@ static long
 mountfix(Chan *c, uchar *op, long n, long maxn)
 {
 	char *name;
-	int nbuf, nname;
+	int nname;
 	Chan *nc;
 	Mhead *mh;
 	Mount *m;
 	uchar *p;
 	int dirlen, rest;
 	long l;
-	uchar *buf, *e;
+	uchar *e;
+	/*
+	 * set and replaced after the waserror below, and its handler's
+	 * Norewrite goes on with the loop and frees buf at the end
+	 */
+	uchar *volatile buf;
+	volatile int nbuf;
 	Dir d;
 
 	p = op;
-- 
2.56.1

